From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: know how far ahead the translation is

Nothing said how far ahead of playback a subtitle being translated
was. After a seek, a new track or a language change, playback went on
at once and the first lines came late, moved to when they arrived.

MODEL::CLead counts it, one per subtitle stream opened to be
translated (a stream only repaired has none): the cues handed in and
not yet shown, by a ticket each, and how far ahead the cues are known -
all of them for a file, as far as the read-ahead has read for a track
read ahead, the last cue received for a stream read as it plays. Every
path that takes a cue gives its ticket back when the cue is shown or
dropped: the worker after delivering it, at a flush, when a seek drops
it from a request or from the cues put back after a larger request
failed, and when it closes; the picture reader likewise, from when a
picture is handed to it until its text goes to the cue stage (which
takes its own ticket first) or is not read. A lead closes with its
stream, so a closed track never holds the next.

CLead::Check(stream, file) says whether playback may go on: every lead
is 15 s ahead of playback - its first cue not yet shown, and how far
it is known, past that - or translated as far as it is known; the
translation gains on playback once it runs, so no more is waited for.
Each is counted on the clock its cues are shown by: a stream's on the
player's clock, a file's on that clock moved by the title's start (a
disc's time offset), both moved by the subtitle delay, as the player
shows them. A cue over already is not waited for: a file's are worked
on from where playback is. A seek starts a lead again; for a stream
its cues are dropped and how far it is read is not known until the
read-ahead says so, with the seek it read after, so what it read
before is not taken. A read-ahead that reads on after a failed read
was taken for the end is no longer at its end. A read-ahead that stops
leaves the stream known as it is read. A stream with no read-ahead
knows no cue ahead of the player's demuxer: after a seek it is ready
at once, and is waited for only for the cues already read - by design,
there is nothing further to wait for.

A lead is waited for only soon after it opens or a seek moves it (20 s,
and for as long as it is asked about after that), and not again once
found ready or given up on (GiveUp()), so a stall later in the film
does not wait for the translation; Check() says when the last lead
waited for moved. All leads share one lock, taken for nothing else.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

